Externalise deployment identity and genericise the runbook

The repository is going public, so it must stop naming one tenant, one
subscription and one set of Entra objects.

Five deployment scripts carried a hardcoded tenant, subscription,
resource group, Entra client, Entra group or registry name. They now
read deploy.env at the repository root, which is gitignored, with
deploy.env.example committed alongside carrying the shape and
placeholders. An explicit parameter still wins; deploy.env only supplies
what was not passed.

The loader fails closed. A deployment script that silently falls back to
a built-in subscription id eventually creates resources in the wrong
account, and that mistake is quiet and expensive, so a missing file or a
placeholder left in it stops the run and says which keys are wrong -
all of them at once, rather than one per five-minute attempt.

workload/docs/INSTALL.md loses 74 occurrences of subscription, tenant,
application, managed identity, workspace, resource group, Key Vault and
hostname values, replaced by angle-bracket names. The public product
domain stays: it is served publicly, documented in the README, and
replacing it would make the runbook harder to follow while protecting
nothing.

Microsoft's own well-known application ids stay too - Power BI, the
Fabric workload client, the Power BI Service - because they are the same
in every directory and a reader needs them verbatim.

The workload manifest returns to its documented placeholders. deploy.ps1
patches the AppId and frontend URL from deploy.env before packaging, so
committing the real values only leaked them.

Also: a control-plane test used the real tenant, client and group ids for
no reason a test requires; SAAS.md, SECURITY.md and CONTRACTS.md named
the registry, the Container Apps domain and the environment's private IP.

Not addressed here, and it matters: these values remain in git history,
in roughly seven commits out of 152. Sanitising the working tree does
not remove them from `git log -p`. Rewriting history is a separate,
destructive decision and is left to the owner.

